WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.640
Every day you make decisions and judgments.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.600
Sometimes you're able to think about them
carefully, but other times you make them on

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.460
the fly using little information.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.380
This is where heuristics come in.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.050
Heuristics are straightforward rules of thumb
that we develop based on our past experiences.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.860
They're cognitive tools that help us make quick decisions or judgments.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.390
Life would be exhausting if we had to deliberate
over every one of the hundreds of choices

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.900
we make every day.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.820
So instead we use our heuristics as shortcuts
to make judgements about the world around us.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.440
For example, rather than spending time
deciding what to wear every day, you might

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.000
have some default outfits.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.260
Or when faced with a lunch menu with too many
options, you may opt for what you've enjoyed

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.900
in the past.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.460
Heuristics aren't about making the perfect
decision or judgment, just about making one

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.760
quickly.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.940
Heuristics play a role in our reasoning about
the broader world too.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.210
As an example, consider the rate of violence
in the world over the past century.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.580
Is the world more or less violent in the past
20 years than previously?

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.640
Heuristic reasoning might lead to think that
the world is more violent today than it has

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:13.880
been in the past.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.180
Every day we're confronted with images of
tragedy in the news and on social media.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.509
We might reasonably assume that the world
is more violent today than ever before, using

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:24.930
what's called an "availability heuristic."

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:29.939
That is, examples of violence that so readily
available, we just naturally assume the world

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:31.930
is more violent today.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.729
But in fact the world is more peaceful today
than ever before in human history.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:40.369
We may hear a lot about violent events but
in terms of raw numbers, fewer people die

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:43.500
today at the hands of other humans beings
than ever before.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.060
So that heuristic about how violent the world
is, is incorrect.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.100
In fact, a slew of other heuristics can easily
to mistaken conclusions.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.560
And it doesn't matter how smart or well educated
you are.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.120
Anyone can place too much trust in the mental
shortcuts they use to make sense of the world.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:03.620
Take this example.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:08.320
Let's say a person tests positive for a rare
disease, one that only one-in-a-thousand people have.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.660
What is the likelihood that he actually has
the disease?

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.560
Most of us would say the likelihood is very
high based on the test results alone.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.380
But what if the results were inaccurate 10%
of the time?

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:23.230
If the false positive rate is 10%, a common
number in medical tests, then it is highly

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.670
unlikely our patient has the disease.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.410
In fact, based on the prevalence of the disease
and the test result, we can be 99% sure he

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.100
doesn't have the disease.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.890
This is because the odds of the getting a
false positive result, one in 10, are much

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:39.880
higher than the odds of actually having the
disease, one in 1,000.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.310
But in multiple studies, physicians routinely
get this wrong, overestimating the likelihood

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.920
that their patient actually has the disease.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.720
Psychologists call this the "representativeness
heuristic."

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.060
People assume an individual case is more representative than it actually is.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.740
Our political views can especially suffer
from an over-reliance on heuristics.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.620
Just consider how we deal with political issues.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.940
We'll often let our political identities, and
our heuristics about how right we think they are,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.400
stand in lieu of important details and
information we need to have an informed viewpoint.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.160
Because our heuristics can so easily lead
us to faulty conclusions, it's important to

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.810
be humble about our views.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:20.300
In light our fallibility, we have to do something
that doesn't come easy, we must recognize

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.660
that the world is an uncertain place and that
our judgements about it are often wrong.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.070
We ought to listen to opinions we may initially
consider wrong or even offensive.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.300
Our intuitions are useful, even necessary
when it comes to making

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.020
quick judgments about the world.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.170
But that doesn't mean they're correct.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:41.120
Recognizing the flawed nature of your thinking
is a bold first step to challenging it.

